eight=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0;border-bottom:10px solid #f5f5f5\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>      <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 10px;line-height:1.5\">\r\n<span class=\"b k\" style=\"font-size:27px;font-family:'gorditamedium',Arial,Helvetica,sans-serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px;line-height:1.5\">  1 big thing: The stories we carry </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>   <tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td> <img src=\"https://images.axios.com/wKtjiIg-PFq6hc84rDrbHcpcarg=/0x0:1920x1080/1920x1080/2026/09/14/1789403733073.jpeg\" width=\"600\" style=\"width:100%\" id=\"item02\" alt=\"Illustration of a tree with the canopy made out of an open book\"> </td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"10\" style=\"height:10px;font-size:0\">\r\n</td>\r\n</tr>\r\n<tr>\r\n<td style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.3\">\r\n<span class=\"b\" style=\"font-size:13px;color:#848484\"> <p>Illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Stock: Getty Images</p> </span>\r\n</td>\r\n</tr><tr>\r\n<td height=\"15\" style=\"height:15px;font-size:0\">&nbsp;</td>\r\n</tr> <tr>\r\n<td class=\"v\" style=\"padding:0 3.5%;line-height:1.75;font-size:17px;font-family:Georgia,serif;color:#222;letter-spacing:-0.1px\"><p><strong>Your family stories are more</strong> than memories. They can be instructions for how to live, <em>Axios' Natalie Daher writes</em>.</p><ul><li><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Stories from parents, grandparents and other older people can help us understand where we came from — and how to handle what comes next.</li></ul><p>🕰️ <strong>Case in point:</strong> Axios Finish Line reader John Jarchow of Brookings, Ore., started writing down family stories in a process he says has been therapeutic and has no end in sight.</p><ul><li><strong>His late parents</strong> had Alzheimer's. His brother is now in memory care. At 76, Jarchow told Axios, \"There's no one else who knows the stories.\"</li><li><strong>What started as</strong> a few memories grew into a 100-plus-page record he plans to self-publish to share with his nieces and nephews, and future generations.</li><li><strong>Along the way,</strong> he realized the project could do more than preserve the past — \"There is a chance to influence the future.\"</li></ul><p>📝 <strong>Between the lines:</strong> Family stories serve different purposes at different stages of life, says Emory psychologist Robyn Fivush, who studies autobiographical memory.</p><ul><li><strong>Shared stories</strong> reinforce family bonds and history.</li><li><strong>Intergenerational stories</strong> — \"let me tell you about when I was a little girl\" — can help younger people understand who they are and what their family values are.</li><li><strong>For older adults,</strong> storytelling can become a kind of life review: What did my life mean? What am I leaving behind?</li></ul><p><strong>The intrigue:</strong> The most useful stories may not be the happiest ones.</p><ul><li><strong>Fivush says hearing</strong> about an older relative's setbacks can give younger people a more realistic model of life.</li><li><strong>If a child only hears</strong> that a grandparent \"lived a charmed life,\" she said, their own struggles can feel like evidence something is wrong with them.</li></ul><p>📱 <strong>The tech angle:</strong> New tools aim to make it easier to preserve those stories without losing the relative's voice.</p><ul><li><strong>Charlie Greene,</strong> founder of family-storytelling platform <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47583278.432951/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cucmVtZW50by5jby8_dXRtX3NvdXJjZT1uZXdzbGV0dGVyJnV0bV9tZWRpdW09ZW1haWwmdXRtX2NhbXBhaWduPW5ld3NsZXR0ZXJfYXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnN0cmVhbT10b3A/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aBdaf45ee9\" target=\"_blank\">Remento</a>, told Axios the company uses AI to suggest prompts, ask follow-up questions and turn recorded conversations into written stories. </li></ul><p><strong>But tech isn't enough.</strong> Remento has found that storytellers are more likely to <em>keep </em>recording when relatives respond to their stories, giving them an audience and showing that their stories matter.</p><ul><li><strong>His advice </strong>—<strong> </strong>get specific. Instead of \"Tell me about your wedding,\" ask what went wrong or what surprised you.</li><li><strong>Greene also recommends</strong> pulling out old photos and asking what was happening when they were taken. </li></ul><p>🌳 <strong>Zoom out: </strong>Susan Bluck, a psychology professor at the University of Florida, says intergenerational storytelling can serve as a way to pass along lessons and values that might help the next generation live \"a good life.\"</p><ul><li><strong>You don't have to be related.</strong> In Bluck's Life Story Lab, students on the research team were <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47583278.432951/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZnJvbnRpZXJzaW4ub3JnL2pvdXJuYWxzL2RldmVsb3BtZW50YWwtcHN5Y2hvbG9neS9hcnRpY2xlcy8xMC4zMzg5L2ZkcHlzLjIwMjYuMTg0NzY4Ni9mdWxsP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9Y2hhdGdwdC5jb20mdXRtX21lZGl1bT1lbWFpbCZ1dG1fY2FtcGFpZ249bmV3c2xldHRlcl9heGlvc2ZpbmlzaGxpbmUmc3RyZWFtPXRvcA/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB966fe72e\" target=\"_blank\">trained to analyze</a> about 200 life-story interviews with older adults who had serious or terminal cancer.</li><li><strong>One student told</strong> <strong>Bluck</strong> the stories helped lessen her sense that she needed to know exactly how her own life would unfold. There were \"so many ways to live a life,\" she realized.</li></ul><p>🧭 <strong>The bottom line:</strong> Older people have already lived through experiences younger people haven't, including loss, relationship changes, career upheaval. Their stories can be an untapped resource.</p><p>💡<strong> Try these <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47583278.432951/aHR0cHM6Ly9kaWduaXR5aW5jYXJlLmNhL2VuL2RpZ25pdHktdGhlcmFweS1hdC1lbmQtb2YtbGlmZS5odG1sP3V0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9wI3F1ZXN0aW9ucw/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB8078d94f\" target=\"_blank\">adapted</a> prompts:</strong></p><ul><li><em>When in your life did you feel most alive?</em></li><li><em>What experience changed the way you see yourself or the world?</em></li><li><em>What's a hard thing you went through — and what did it teach you?</em></li><li><em>What are you most proud of, and why did it matter to you?</em></li><li><em>What have you learned about life that you'd want to pass on to someone younger?</em></li></ul><p>💌 <strong>Your turn: </strong>What family story has stayed with you? Write to us at <a href=\"mailto:finishline@axios.com\" target=\"_blank\">finishline@axios.com</a> with a couple of lines about a story from an older person that helped you through something in your own life. We may feature it in a future edition.</p></td>\r\n</tr> <tr><td align=\"center\" style=\"padding:20px 3.5% 25px\">   <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47583278.432951/aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cuZmFjZWJvb2suY29tL3NoYXJlci5waHA_dT1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtZGExMDJhZjUtMGVjMC00ZGYyLTg4MmMtYzc3YzM3OTgzMTU5Lmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM0QxJTI2dXRtX3Rlcm0lM0RmYnNvY2lhbHNoYXJlJTIzc3RvcnkxJnV0bV9zb3VyY2U9bmV3c2xldHRlciZ1dG1fbWVkaXVtPWVtYWlsJnV0bV9jYW1wYWlnbj1uZXdzbGV0dGVyX2F4aW9zZmluaXNobGluZSZzdHJlYW09dG9w/6a14a774a4e0095e0a03c94aB3e73816a\" style=\"display:inline-block\"><img width=\"32\" height=\"32\" style=\"border:0\" src=\"https://static.axios.com/img/axios-site/newsletters/logos/footer/share-facebook-circle-border.png\" alt=\"Share on Facebook\"></a> <a href=\"https://link.axios.com/click/47583278.432951/aHR0cHM6Ly90d2l0dGVyLmNvbS9pbnRlbnQvdHdlZXQ_dGV4dD0xJTIwYmlnJTIwdGhpbmc6JTIwVGhlJTIwc3RvcmllcyUyMHdlJTIwY2FycnkmaGFzaHRhZ3M9YXhpb3NmaW5pc2hsaW5lJnVybD1odHRwczovL3d3dy5heGlvcy5jb20vbmV3c2xldHRlcnMvYXhpb3MtZmluaXNoLWxpbmUtZGExMDJhZjUtMGVjMC00ZGYyLTg4MmMtYzc3YzM3OTgzMTU5Lmh0bWw_Y2h1bmslM